course: dictionary
lesson: 743
title: A word with two jobs: bath
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Bath can be a noun or a verb, so read the sentence to find out which one it is. As a noun, a bath is a long container you fill with water and sit in to wash your body. Have a bath (UK) or take a bath (US) means washing yourself in one.
explain: «A girl in Sydney runs her bath with the cold water first, then the hot, and an adult tests it with an elbow before she gets in, so she never gets scalded.» Here bath is a noun, and run means fill with water. The clues cold first, then hot, and tests it with an elbow tell you the water is being checked to keep her safe.
explain: In UK English, bath can also be a verb meaning to wash a baby or child in a bath. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing you sit in, or an action of washing? That question settles most cases.
fact: Bath (noun) means a long container you fill with water and sit in to wash your body.
fact: Have a bath (UK) or take a bath (US) means washing your body in one.
fact: Bath (verb, UK) means to wash a baby or child in a bath.
fact: «Scalded» means hurt by very hot water or steam.
fact: Baths and showers keep skin clean and remove germs and sweat.
exercise: «A girl in Sydney runs her bath with the cold water first, then the hot.» What does run her bath mean? | Dry it with a towel | Leave it empty | *Fill it with water | Clean the floor next to it
exercise: «Food-factory workers often walk through a shoe-cleaning bath, so no hair or germs get into the food.» What does the shoe-cleaning bath do? | It warms the workers' feet | It holds spare hairnets | *It cleans the shoes as people walk through | It makes soup for lunch
exercise: In the UK, to bath a baby means to do what? | *Wash the baby in a bath | Put the baby to bed | Dry the baby with a towel | Feed the baby milk
test: «The Coriolis effect is too weak to decide which way water drains from a bath or a sink.» What does this sentence say about the Coriolis effect? | It is a kind of bathroom tap | It always makes water swirl to the left | It is strong enough to lift a bath | *It is too weak to decide which way water drains
test: «A child who is shivering hard, pale and mumbling must be warmed slowly, never dumped in a hot bath.» What should happen to this child? | Leave the child outside to shiver | *Warm the child slowly | Give the child a cold shower | Put the child straight into a very hot bath
exam: «Food-factory workers... often walk through a shoe-cleaning bath, so no hair or germs get into the food.» Why do the workers do this? | *So no hair or germs get into the food | To give the food a bath-time smell | To make their shoes shine for a photo | To cool down after lunch
